\chapter{Detecting changes with HAR files}
\label{HAR}

FBF's API is private.
FBF changes it when their web app needs a change, and nobody tells us; the
same goes for the launch, which depends on Canvas too.
An earlier attempt at this package shows how quickly that bites: a year on,
its authentication no longer matched the API, and some attribute names it
documented had never matched it.
So we plan for change.
This chapter gives us a way to see \emph{what} changed, using nothing but a
browser.

\section{The idea: compare shapes, not values}

A browser can save everything it sends and receives as a HAR file (HTTP
Archive): open the developer tools, open the FBF assignment, save.
A HAR shows exactly what the web app does today, so comparing a fresh HAR
with an old one shows what changed.
But we cannot keep HAR files around, let alone commit them: they hold live
session cookies and tokens, and the names and texts of students.

What changes in an API, though, is its \emph{shape}: the hops of the launch,
the endpoints, the form fields and cookies, the resource types with their
attribute and relationship names.
Values change every day without breaking anything; a renamed attribute
breaks us however its values look.
So we reduce a HAR to its shape, which holds names only and no values, and
compare shapes.
The shape of a known-good capture is committed as
[[doc/fbf-api-shape.txt]], and [[fbf har-diff]] compares a fresh capture with
it.

A shape is a text file of one record per line, which keeps it readable and
lets [[git diff]] show its history.
For example:
\begin{verbatim}
launch POST api.feedbackfruits.com/v1/lti/login 307 fields=client_id,...
endpoint GET api.eu.feedbackfruits.com/v1/tasks/{id} 200 types=...
type multimedia-tasks attribute deadline-at
type multimedia-tasks relationship progresses
\end{verbatim}
The [[launch]] records keep the order of the requests, since the order of
the hops matters; the other records are sorted.

