\section{Which requests count}

A HAR of opening an FBF assignment has a few hundred entries: scripts,
fonts, images, analytics, a help-desk widget.
Only two kinds of entries matter to us.
\begin{description}
\item[Launch hops] are the page loads and form posts on Canvas and on FBF:
  what the browser \emph{navigates} to, in the page or in the tool's
  iframe.
  Browsers mark those requests with the header [[Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate]],
  while an image or a script is fetched in another mode.
  A HAR without that header (an old browser) leaves us a guess: redirects and
  HTML responses.
  The guess admits a redirecting image now and then, which only adds noise.
  A path under [[/lti/]] is no sign of a hop, since FBF's API has JSON
  endpoints such as [[/v1/lti/courses/<id>]] too.
\item[API calls] are JSON responses from an FBF host.
\end{description}
Everything else we skip.
We recognise FBF by its domain and Canvas by the host of the first entry,
which is the Canvas page the capture started on.
CORS preflight requests ([[OPTIONS]]) carry no information of their own, so
we skip those too.

<<constants>>=
FBF_DOMAIN = "feedbackfruits.com"
<<functions>>=
def is_fbf_host(host):
    """Tell whether `host` belongs to FeedbackFruits."""
    return host == FBF_DOMAIN or host.endswith("." + FBF_DOMAIN)


def mime_type(entry):
    """Return the MIME type of the response of a HAR `entry`, lowercase."""
    mime = entry["response"].get("content", {}).get("mimeType") or ""
    return mime.split(";")[0].strip().lower()


def is_launch_hop(entry, canvas_host):
    """Tell whether a HAR `entry` is a page load or form post of the launch."""
    request = entry["request"]
    host = urllib.parse.urlsplit(request["url"]).hostname or ""
    if request["method"] not in ("GET", "POST"):
        return False
    if not (is_fbf_host(host) or host == canvas_host):
        return False
    mode = request_header(entry, "sec-fetch-mode")
    if mode:
        return mode == "navigate"
    status = entry["response"]["status"]
    return 300 <= status < 400 or mime_type(entry) == "text/html"


def request_header(entry, name):
    """Return the request header `name` (lowercase) of a HAR `entry`."""
    return next((header["value"] for header
                 in entry["request"].get("headers", [])
                 if header["name"].lower() == name), None)


def is_api_call(entry):
    """Tell whether a HAR `entry` is a JSON response from an FBF host."""
    request = entry["request"]
    host = urllib.parse.urlsplit(request["url"]).hostname or ""
    return (request["method"] != "OPTIONS" and is_fbf_host(host)
            and mime_type(entry).endswith("json"))
@


\section{Paths without identifiers}

The same endpoint appears with a different id in every request:
[[/v1/tasks/63d1...]] and [[/v1/tasks/1322...]] are one endpoint.
So we replace the identifying segments of a path by placeholders: UUIDs by
[[{id}]], numbers (Canvas ids) by [[{n}]], long hexadecimal strings by
[[{hex}]].
Any other segment that does not look like a fixed word becomes [[{x}]].
A fixed word is lowercase and short, like the words in FBF's and Canvas's
paths; a random token such as Canvas's thumbnail keys mixes cases.
That last rule is a privacy guard as much as a normalisation: whatever odd
value ends up in a path, a name or an email, it never reaches the shape.

<<imports>>=
import re
import urllib.parse
<<constants>>=
SEGMENT_PATTERNS = [
    (re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}",
                re.IGNORECASE), "{id}"),
    (re.compile(r"[0-9]+"), "{n}"),
    (re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{16,}", re.IGNORECASE), "{hex}"),
]
FIXED_SEGMENT = re.compile(r"[a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,39}")
<<functions>>=
def template_segment(segment):
    """Return a path segment with its identifier replaced by a placeholder."""
    decoded = urllib.parse.unquote(segment)
    for pattern, placeholder in SEGMENT_PATTERNS:
        if pattern.fullmatch(decoded):
            return placeholder
    if "/" in decoded:
        return urllib.parse.quote(
            "/".join(template_segment(part) for part in decoded.split("/")),
            safe="{}")
    return segment if FIXED_SEGMENT.fullmatch(decoded) else "{x}"


def template_url(url):
    """Return `host/path` of `url` with identifiers replaced."""
    parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url)
    path = "/".join(template_segment(s) if s else s
                    for s in parts.path.split("/"))
    return f"{parts.hostname}{path}"
@

FBF's course path contains an encoded slash, [[lti%2Fcourse]], which we
keep: it is a fixed word, not an identifier.

