\section{The shape of one entry}

For each kind of entry we keep the names that our code depends on.

\subsection{Launch hops}

A hop is the request (method, templated URL), the status, the names of the
posted form fields, the names of the cookies the response sets, and the
forms in the response with their actions and field names.
The fields of the forms in the response are what the next hop posts, so
they are what [[fbf.launch]] looks for.

<<functions>>=
def launch_record(entry):
    """Return the shape record of a launch hop."""
    request, response = entry["request"], entry["response"]
    record = (f"launch {request['method']} {template_url(request['url'])} "
              f"{response['status']}")
    fields = sorted({p["name"] for p in
                     (request.get("postData") or {}).get("params", [])})
    if fields:
        record += " fields=" + ",".join(fields)
    cookies = sorted(set_cookie_names(response))
    if cookies:
        record += " cookies=" + ",".join(cookies)
    for form in response_forms(response):
        record += (f" form={template_url(form.action)}:"
                   + ",".join(sorted(form.fields)))
    return record
@

A cookie's name is the part of a [[Set-Cookie]] header before the first
[[=]]; the value and attributes after it are not ours to keep.

<<functions>>=
def set_cookie_names(response):
    """Return the names of the cookies a HAR `response` sets."""
    return {header["value"].split("=", 1)[0].strip()
            for header in response.get("headers", [])
            if header["name"].lower() == "set-cookie"}
@

The forms come from the same parser as the launch uses, so the shape sees
the forms the way our code sees them.
A relative action is resolved against nothing, which leaves its path; that
is good enough for a shape.

<<imports>>=
from fbf.launch import parse_page
<<functions>>=
def response_forms(response):
    """Return the forms with an action in the HTML of a HAR `response`."""
    content = response.get("content", {})
    if not (content.get("mimeType") or "").startswith("text/html"):
        return []
    return [form for form in parse_page(content.get("text") or "").forms
            if form.action.startswith("http")]
@

\subsection{API calls}

An API call is keyed by method and templated URL.
We record the names of the query parameters, the status, and what came back:
the types in [[data]] for a JSON:API document, the top-level keys for any
other JSON.
The types in [[included]] depend on the [[include]] parameter, which the
query names already show.
The resources themselves, in [[data]] and [[included]], each give us type
records with their attribute and relationship names.

<<imports>>=
import json
<<functions>>=
def api_records(entry):
    """Return the endpoint record and the type records of an API call."""
    request, response = entry["request"], entry["response"]
    query = sorted({p["name"] for p in request.get("queryString", [])})
    record = (f"endpoint {request['method']} {template_url(request['url'])} "
              f"{response['status']}")
    if query:
        record += " query=" + ",".join(query)
    try:
        body = json.loads(response.get("content", {}).get("text") or "")
    except ValueError:
        return [record], []
    if not isinstance(body, dict):
        return [record], []
    resources = jsonapi_resources(body)
    if "data" in body:
        types = sorted({resource["type"] for resource
                        in jsonapi_resources({"data": body["data"]})})
        record += " types=" + ",".join(types)
    else:
        record += " keys=" + ",".join(sorted(body))
    return [record], [line for resource in resources
                      for line in type_records(resource)]
@

The resources of a document are those in [[data]], one or a list, and those
in [[included]].

<<functions>>=
def jsonapi_resources(document):
    """Return all resources in the `data` and `included` of `document`."""
    data = document.get("data")
    data = data if isinstance(data, list) else [data] if data else []
    return [resource for resource in data + document.get("included", [])
            if isinstance(resource, dict) and "type" in resource]


def type_records(resource):
    """Return the type records for the names in a JSON:API `resource`."""
    kind = resource["type"]
    return ([f"type {kind} attribute {name}"
             for name in resource.get("attributes") or {}]
            + [f"type {kind} relationship {name}"
               for name in resource.get("relationships") or {}])
@


\section{The shape of a capture}

The shape of a HAR is its launch records in order, followed by the sorted,
unique endpoint and type records.
An endpoint called several times may have answered with different statuses
or types; we keep one record per distinct answer, which shows such variation
instead of hiding it.

We accept several HAR files and unite their shapes, because one capture
rarely visits every view.
The reference shape grows by adding captures of new views.
The launch, however, is the same in every capture, so we take it from the
first capture that has one.

<<functions>>=
def har_shape(hars):
    """Return the shape (list of records) of the HAR documents `hars`."""
    launch, endpoints, types = [], set(), set()
    for har in hars:
        entries = har["log"]["entries"]
        canvas_host = (urllib.parse.urlsplit(entries[0]["request"]["url"])
                       .hostname if entries else None)
        hops = [launch_record(entry) for entry in entries
                if is_launch_hop(entry, canvas_host)]
        launch = launch or hops
        for entry in entries:
            if is_api_call(entry):
                endpoint_lines, type_lines = api_records(entry)
                endpoints.update(endpoint_lines)
                types.update(type_lines)
    return launch + sorted(endpoints) + sorted(types)
@

A test HAR needs only a few entries to cover the selection: a Canvas page
