\section{Comparing shapes}

A new capture is compared with the reference shape.
Not every difference means trouble: a capture that did not open the
peer-review view lacks the peer-review endpoints, yet nothing changed.
So we sort the differences by what they tell us.
\begin{description}
\item[Changed launch.] Any difference in the launch records, since every
  launch goes through the same hops.
\item[Removed.] A type or endpoint seen in both captures that lost an
  attribute, a relationship or a status.
  This is what breaks code.
\item[Added.] Names that are new for a type or endpoint seen in both.
  Usually harmless, and sometimes the replacement of a removed name.
\item[Not seen, new.] Types and endpoints only in one of the shapes.
  Either a view that one capture did not visit, or a rename; the removed
  and added lists of the same capture tell which.
\end{description}
The launch is compared as a sequence, with [[difflib]], since an inserted
redirect shifts all later hops.
Types are compared per name: we group the type records by type and compare
the sets of names.

<<imports>>=
import collections
import difflib
<<functions>>=
def group_records(records, kind):
    """Group the `kind` records by their key (type or endpoint).

    Type records are grouped by type name, with the attribute and
    relationship names as members.  Endpoint records are grouped by method
    and URL, with the rest of the record as members.
    """
    groups = collections.defaultdict(set)
    for record in records:
        if not record.startswith(kind + " "):
            continue
        words = record.split(" ")
        if kind == "type":
            groups[words[1]].add(" ".join(words[2:]))
        else:
            groups[" ".join(words[1:3])].add(" ".join(words[3:]))
    return groups
@

The comparison returns a dictionary of the four kinds of differences, each a
list of lines, so that the command can print them and the tests can check
them.

<<functions>>=
def shape_diff(reference, current):
    """Compare two shapes; return the differences sorted by kind."""
    diff = {"launch": [], "removed": [], "added": [],
            "not seen": [], "new": []}
    old_launch = [r for r in reference if r.startswith("launch ")]
    new_launch = [r for r in current if r.startswith("launch ")]
    if old_launch and new_launch and old_launch != new_launch:
        diff["launch"] = list(difflib.unified_diff(
            old_launch, new_launch, "reference", "capture", lineterm="", n=0))
    for kind in ("endpoint", "type"):
        old = group_records(reference, kind)
        new = group_records(current, kind)
        for key in sorted(old.keys() | new.keys()):
            if key not in new:
                diff["not seen"].append(f"{kind} {key}")
            elif key not in old:
                diff["new"].append(f"{kind} {key}")
            else:
                diff["removed"] += [f"{kind} {key} {name}"
                                    for name in sorted(old[key] - new[key])]
                diff["added"] += [f"{kind} {key} {name}"
                                  for name in sorted(new[key] - old[key])]
    return diff
@

The test that matters most: a renamed attribute must show up as removed
(and the new name as added), while a type the capture never saw must not.

<<test functions>>=
def test_shape_diff_flags_renamed_attribute():
  reference = har_shape([sample_har("title")]) + ["type groups attribute name"]
  diff = shape_diff(reference, har_shape([sample_har("name")]))
  assert diff["removed"] == ["type tasks attribute title"]
  assert diff["added"] == ["type tasks attribute name"]
  assert diff["not seen"] == ["type groups"]
  assert diff["launch"] == [] and diff["new"] == []

def test_shape_diff_flags_changed_launch():
  current = [r.replace(" 302", " 307") for r in har_shape([sample_har()])]
  diff = shape_diff(har_shape([sample_har()]), current)
  assert any(line.startswith("+launch POST") and "307" in line
             for line in diff["launch"])
