\section{Updating the reference}

We cannot keep the HAR files, so the reference must be updated from its own
records and a new capture.
Uniting the two would be wrong: after a rename, the old name would stay in
the reference, and every later diff would report it as removed.
The new capture is the truth for what it saw, and the reference for what it
did not see.
So the update replaces the records of every type and endpoint the capture
saw and keeps the others; the launch comes from the capture if it has one.

<<functions>>=
def merge_shapes(reference, current):
    """Update the shape `reference` with what the shape `current` saw."""
    launch = ([r for r in current if r.startswith("launch ")]
              or [r for r in reference if r.startswith("launch ")])
    seen = {kind: group_records(current, kind)
            for kind in ("endpoint", "type")}
    kept = [record for record in reference
            if not record.startswith("launch ")
            and not any(record.startswith(f"{kind} {key} ")
                        for kind in seen for key in seen[kind])]
    updated = [r for r in current if not r.startswith("launch ")]
    endpoints = sorted({r for r in kept + updated if r.startswith("endpoint ")})
    types = sorted({r for r in kept + updated if r.startswith("type ")})
    return launch + endpoints + types
@

The update must drop a renamed attribute and keep a type the capture never
saw.

<<test functions>>=
def test_merge_shapes_replaces_seen_and_keeps_unseen():
  reference = har_shape([sample_har("title")]) + ["type groups attribute name"]
  merged = merge_shapes(reference, har_shape([sample_har("name")]))
  assert "type tasks attribute name" in merged
  assert "type tasks attribute title" not in merged
  assert "type groups attribute name" in merged
  current = har_shape([sample_har("name")])
  assert merged[:3] == current[:3]
  assert merged[3:] == sorted(current[3:] + ["type groups attribute name"])
@


\section{The commands}

Two commands make this usable from the shell.
[[fbf har-shape]] prints the shape of one or more HAR files.
With [[--update]] it instead merges them into the reference file as above,
which is how the reference follows FBF.
Redirecting its output to [[doc/fbf-api-shape.txt]] starts a new
reference.
[[fbf har-diff]] compares a HAR with the reference.
It exits with status~1 when the launch changed or something was removed,
so that it can guard a script.
A removed name that [[fbf]] reads, according to [[REQUIRED]] of
\cref{Schema}, is marked with [[!!]]: that is the change to fix first.

Both work without Canvas, and their arguments are files, so they follow the
command convention of [[canvaslms]], [[(config, canvas, args)]], and ignore
the first two.
That lets the [[fbf]] command and, later, [[canvaslms]] register them as
subcommands unchanged.

<<imports>>=
import pathlib
import sys
from fbf.schema import REQUIRED
<<constants>>=
REFERENCE_SHAPE = "doc/fbf-api-shape.txt"
SHAPE_HEADER = """\
# The shape of FeedbackFruits' launch and API, made by `fbf har-shape`.
# Names only, no values.  Compare a fresh capture with `fbf har-diff`.
"""
<<functions>>=
def read_hars(paths):
    """Return the HAR documents in the files `paths`."""
    return [json.loads(pathlib.Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            for path in paths]


def read_shape(path):
    """Return the records of a shape file, without comments."""
    return [line for line in
            pathlib.Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
            if line and not line.startswith("#")]


def har_shape_command(config, canvas, args):
    """Print the shape of `args.har`, or merge it into `args.update`."""
    shape = har_shape(read_hars(args.har))
    if args.update:
        shape = merge_shapes(read_shape(args.update), shape)
        pathlib.Path(args.update).write_text(
            SHAPE_HEADER + "\n".join(shape) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    else:
        print(SHAPE_HEADER + "\n".join(shape))


def har_diff_command(config, canvas, args):
    """Compare the HAR files `args.har` with the shape `args.reference`."""
    diff = shape_diff(read_shape(args.reference),
                      har_shape(read_hars(args.har)))
    for kind, lines in diff.items():
        if lines:
            print(f"{kind}:")
            print("\n".join(("!!" if line in REQUIRED else "  ") + line
                            for line in lines))
    if diff["launch"] or diff["removed"]:
        sys.exit(1)
@

Let's verify the command end to end on files: a capture that renamed an
attribute we require must exit with status~1 and mark the removal.

<<test functions>>=
def test_har_diff_command_marks_required_removal(tmp_path, capsys):
  import argparse, pytest
  from fbf.schema import requires
  requires("tasks", attributes=["title"])
  reference = tmp_path / "shape.txt"
  reference.write_text("\n".join(har_shape([sample_har("title")])))
  capture = tmp_path / "new.har"
  capture.write_text(json.dumps(sample_har("name")))
  args = argparse.Namespace(har=[str(capture)], reference=str(reference))
  with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as exit:
    har_diff_command(None, None, args)
  assert exit.value.code == 1
  assert "!!type tasks attribute title" in capsys.readouterr().out
