\section{When FeedbackFruits changes: the procedure}
\label{Recovery}

This section is the procedure to follow when the package breaks, and also
when we want data from a view we have never extracted.
Both start the same way: look at what the web app does today.

\begin{enumerate}
\item\label{RecoverySymptom}
  \textbf{Notice.}
  The signs of change are a [[LaunchError]] (it names the hop), an
  [[APIError]] or a missing attribute in an extractor, or a drift report from
  the live tests.
  For a new extractor, the sign is simply that we want data we do not have.

\item\label{RecoveryCapture}
  \textbf{Capture.}
  In Firefox (or Chrome), open the developer tools and their Network tab.
  Enable \enquote{Persist Logs} (Chrome: \enquote{Preserve log}), so that
  the redirects of the launch stay in the list, and \enquote{Disable Cache},
  so that every response is captured with its content.
  Then open the FBF assignment from its Canvas module, as the reference
  capture did, and click through the views whose data we need: the results
  overview, a student's received and given reviews, the comments.
  Finally save everything: right-click in the list, \enquote{Save All As
  HAR}.
  Save the file outside the repository, in [[~/private/fbf-hars/]], named by
  date and view.
  The repository's [[.gitignore]] refuses [[*.har]] as a second guard.

\item\label{RecoveryDiagnose}
  \textbf{Diagnose.}
  Run [[fbf har-diff ~/private/fbf-hars/<file>.har]] in the repository.
  The launch differences say which hop changed; the removed and added names
  say what was renamed; the new endpoints and types are where to look for
  new data.
  To see values, which the shape leaves out, open the HAR in the browser's
  network tab rather than printing it: the HAR is full of personal data.

\item\label{RecoveryFix}
  \textbf{Fix.}
  Change the launch (\cref{Launch}) or the extractors to match.
  Re-record the affected tests and review the recordings' diff, then run the
  privacy scan on them.
  Update the reference with [[fbf har-shape --update <file>.har]], which
  replaces what the capture saw and keeps the views it did not visit.
  Commit the code, the shape and the recordings together, so that the
  history shows what FBF changed and how we followed.

\item\label{RecoveryCleanUp}
  \textbf{Clean up.}
  The HAR holds live session cookies: a Canvas session and an FBF refresh
  token that is valid for 14~days.
  Log out of Canvas and FBF to end those sessions, and delete the HAR when
  the fix is committed.
\end{enumerate}
